\description{
The biotype split used by the workflow (protein coding versus retained
intron, nonsense-mediated decay, ...) is a proxy. These functions make
productivity a computed, graded property of every transcript and
summarise it per gene:
\itemize{
\item \code{transcript_productivity()} classifies each assayed transcript as
\code{"productive"} (complete ORF, no predicted NMD, same ORF as the
canonical isoform), \code{"altered_productive"} (complete ORF, no NMD, but
a different ORF than the canonical isoform: alternative start, in-frame
change, frameshift or stop in novel sequence), \code{"unproductive"} (no
ORF, premature stop codon by the 50-nt rule, no stop codon, an
unproductive biotype such as \code{nonsense_mediated_decay},
\code{retained_intron}, \code{protein_coding_CDS_not_defined}, or a longest ORF
without coding potential), \code{"noncoding"} (transcripts of genes without
any coding isoform) or \code{"uncertain"} (incomplete CDS annotation without
a premature stop, a credible ORF unrelated to the canonical one, FANTOM
\code{uncertain_coding}).
The \code{reason} column explains each call and \code{source} tells whether it
came from the sequence (\code{"sequence"}, when the annotation has
transcript sequences, see \code{\link[=extract_transcript_sequences]{extract_transcript_sequences()}} and
\code{\link[=transcript_orfs]{transcript_orfs()}}), the annotation (\code{"annotation"}) or external
evidence (\code{"evidence"}). Evidence columns take precedence, in this
order: a \code{productivity_evidence} column added with
\code{\link[=add_annotation_columns]{add_annotation_columns()}}, a validated NMD exon (\code{\link[=add_nmd_exons]{add_nmd_exons()}}),
a Ribo-seq ORF matching the transcript's ORF (\code{\link[=add_riboseq_orfs]{add_riboseq_orfs()}}),
an APPRIS principal isoform (\code{\link[=add_appris]{add_appris()}}).
\item \code{productive_fraction()} is the share of a gene's expression carried by
productive (and altered productive) isoforms, per sample or per group,
with the difference between the last and the first group level. A
gene whose expression is flat while its productive fraction drops is a
post-transcriptional regulation call that gene-level differential
expression misses. With inferential replicates (\code{inf_reps}, Salmon
bootstraps or Gibbs samples) it reports the uncertainty of each
fraction, and with a \code{reference} (\code{\link[=gtex_reference]{gtex_reference()}}) the difference to
the healthy-tissue baseline.
\item \code{productivity_fractions()} splits the gene's expression over all
productivity classes and, for unproductive isoforms, over the
mechanism (\code{nmd}, \code{retained_intron}, \code{no_orf}, \code{non_stop}, \code{other}).
\item \code{test_productive_fraction()} tests the change in productive fraction
between two groups on counts, with a quasi-binomial model per gene
(pooled proportions per group, Pearson over-dispersion, t test on the
log odds ratio, BH adjustment).
\item \code{run_productivity_enrichment()} ranks genes by the change in
productive fraction between two groups and runs gene set enrichment
(\code{fgsea}) on gene symbols, which captures regulated unproductive
splicing (AS-NMD) directly.
\item \code{poison_exons()} lists exons that occur only in unproductive isoforms
of genes that also have productive isoforms and that do not overlap
any exon of a productive isoform (cassette poison / NMD exons, as
opposed to retained introns or alternative splice sites);
\code{\link[=plot_genomic_context]{plot_genomic_context()}} outlines them with \code{highlight_poison_exons}.
\item \code{benchmark_productivity()} measures the rules against the annotation
biotypes: recall of \code{nonsense_mediated_decay} and \code{retained_intron}
transcripts by the sequence/structure rules alone, and the share of
\code{protein_coding} and canonical transcripts called productive.
\item \code{productivity_palette()} gives colours for the classes.
}
}
